.TH PAKU 1 2026-11-23 "paku 0.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
paku \- a small package-management wrapper around an AUR helper and pacman
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paku
.I subcommand
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B paku
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B paku
gives everyday package management on Arch Linux and its derivatives a smaller set of subcommands.
It uses an AUR helper,
.BR paru (8),
.BR yay (8)
or
.BR pikaur (1),
so the AUR is included: the one chosen (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ),
or else the first of them on the PATH, in that order. Below,
.B paru
stands for the helper in use. Without one,
.BR pacman (8),
with
.BR sudo (8)
for the operations that change the system (not when run as root), and the official repositories only.
Commands run directly, never through a shell, and
.B paku
exits with their exit code.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BR upgrade ", " up " [" \-\-no\-flatpak ]
.B paru \-Syu
(or
.BR "sudo pacman \-Syu" ),
then
.B flatpak upgrade
if flatpak is installed and the system upgrade succeeded.
With
.BR \-\-no\-flatpak ,
only the system upgrade.
.TP
.BR maintain ", " cu ", " full ", " full\-upgrade " [" \-\-dry\-run "] [" \-\-no\-flatpak "] [" \-\-devel ]
With
.B cachy\-update
installed, runs it with the arguments as given.
Otherwise runs paku's own flow: the pending updates; the Arch Linux news not shown before (with
.BR curl );
the upgrade, after a [Y/n], as
.BR upgrade ;
orphans
.RB ( "sudo pacman \-Rns" );
unused Flatpak runtimes;
the package cache (first the empty
.I download\-*
directories pacman left, as in
.BR clean ,
with no question; then
.B "sudo paccache \-rk3"
and
.BR \-ruk0 ),
then the helper's caches;
.I .pacnew
and
.I .pacsave
files
.RB ( "pacdiff \-\-sudo" );
failed units and a journal over 1 GiB, as information only;
after an upgrade, the services to restart
.RB ( checkservices ,
from archlinux\-contrib),
all or none, never the display managers (with the unit behind
.IR display\-manager.service ),
the service of the current login session, D\-Bus, logind, the network services, the gettys or
.I user@
units;
and a reboot when the running kernel is no longer installed
(as the user from a session on a local seat; without one, over ssh for example, through
.BR "sudo systemctl reboot" ).
Every step but the upgrade asks [y/N].
The last line says how it went, and ends with
.I "; reboot recommended (...)"
when a new kernel wasn't booted or services were kept running on old files.
The tools of the flow come with paku's dependencies (pacman\-contrib, fakeroot, archlinux\-contrib, curl);
a step whose tool is missing anyway is skipped with a note naming the package to install.
Without a terminal, or with
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
every step only lists what it would do, the upgrade included; the services check, which only means
something after the upgrade, is listed as the command it would run then.
.B \-\-no\-flatpak
leaves Flatpak out;
.B \-\-devel
goes to the helper.
.B \-\-noconfirm
is never passed.
The flow follows arch\-update 4.4.7 (https://github.com/Antiz96/arch\-update), reimplemented with no code of it;
it differs in the [y/N] defaults and in the information steps.
.TP
.BR check ", " co
Lists pending updates:
.B cachy\-update \-\-list
when installed (which can include Flatpak updates), otherwise
.B paru \-Qu
(without paru,
.BR checkupdates ,
else
.BR "pacman \-Qu" ),
packages only, with no Flatpak.
.TP
.BR update ", " refresh
.B paru \-Sy
(or
.BR "sudo pacman \-Sy" ).
.TP
.BR search ", " s " " \fIpkg\fR ...
.B paru \-Ss
(or
.BR "pacman \-Ss" ).
.TP
.BR install ", " add ", " i " " \fIpkg\fR ...
.B paru \-S
(or
.BR "sudo pacman \-S" ).
.TP
.BR remove ", " rm ", " uninstall " " \fIpkg\fR ...
.B paru \-Rns
(or
.BR "sudo pacman \-Rns" ).
.TP
.BR info " " \fIpkg\fR ...
.B paru \-Si
(or
.BR "pacman \-Si" ,
then
.B pacman \-Qi
if the package is not in the repositories).
.TP
.BR owns " " \fIfile\fR ...
.BR "pacman \-Qo" .
.TP
.BR files " " \fIpkg\fR ...
.BR "pacman \-Ql" .
.TP
.BR list ", " explicit
.BR "pacman \-Qe" .
.TP
.BR aur ", " aur\-list
.BR "pacman \-Qm" .
.TP
.BR aur\-audit ", " aur\-sec ", " aur\-security
Fetches the Arch Linux AUR compromised-package list with
.B curl
and checks the installed foreign packages against it.
.TP
.B clean
.B paru \-Sc
(or
.BR "sudo pacman \-Sc" ).
First removes the empty
.I download\-*
directories pacman 7 leaves in the cache directories
.RB ( "pacman\-conf CacheDir" )
after installing a local package file (how the AUR helpers install: one more at every AUR install), and in the
.I sync
directory of the database after a failed sync; through
.B sudo
(not as root), and not while pacman's
.I db.lck
exists. A directory with a file in it (a download under way or interrupted) is kept; that is the one
.B \-Sc
reports with "Error reading fd 8", to remove by hand once no pacman is running.
This works around pacman issue #297
.RI ( https://gitlab.archlinux.org/pacman/pacman/-/issues/297 ),
until pacman fixes it.
.TP
.BR autoremove ", " orphans
Removes the orphans
.RB ( "pacman \-Qdtq" )
with
.B \-Rns.
.TP
.BR completions " " bash | zsh | fish
Prints the completion script for the shell.
.TP
.B helper
Prints the tool the other subcommands use, as one word:
.BR paru ", " yay ", " pikaur " or " pacman
(see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
A setting that can't be followed is warned about on stderr.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The helper comes from the
.B PAKU_HELPER
environment variable or, without it, from a
.B helper
line in the config file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# auto, paru, yay, pikaur or pacman
helper = yay
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B auto
(the default, also with no file) takes the first helper installed, in the order paru, yay, pikaur, and
pacman without any.
.B pacman
uses pacman even when a helper is installed. A chosen helper that isn't installed, or an unknown value,
gets a warning on stderr and counts as
.BR auto .
Only the subcommands that run the package tool read the setting.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PAKU_HELPER
The helper, as in the config file; it wins over the file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/paku/config
The config file
.RI ( ~/.config/paku/config
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset or not absolute). Lines are
.IR "key = value" ,
the value may be quoted, and
.B #
starts a comment.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/paku/last\-news
The newest Arch news item
.B maintain
showed
.RI ( ~/.local/state/paku/last\-news ).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/paku\-maintain.lock
The lock of
.B maintain
(else
.IR /run/user/UID ,
else
.IR ~/.cache/paku ).
As root, the lock is
.I /run/paku\-maintain.lock
and the news state
.IR /var/lib/paku/last\-news .
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit code of the command run. 1 with no subcommand, an unknown one or a missing argument;
.B aur\-audit
exits 1 when an installed package is on the list or the list can't be fetched.
.B maintain
(its own flow) exits 0 when done, steps skipped or answered no included; 1 on bad usage; 2 when a step
failed (the next steps still ran); 17 when another
.B paku maintain
is running; 130 on Ctrl\-C, which stops the flow at once. On a Ctrl\-C, at a question or during a command,
paku prints
.B paku maintain: stopped (Ctrl\-C)
and then dies of SIGINT itself, so a shell running it stops too, the way it would for any command stopped
by Ctrl\-C; the shell reports 130 in
.BR $? .
A transaction pacman has started is left to pacman's own handling of the signal: a Ctrl\-C during the package
transfer reaches pacman through sudo, and a calling script sees exit 130 (it may not get the signal itself). A caller that wants to
carry on after a Ctrl\-C has to trap SIGINT itself (for example
.BR "trap : INT" ),
not ignore it: paku inherits an ignored SIGINT and then can't be stopped by Ctrl\-C at all.
When the upgrade fails, the steps that remove or restart something are skipped; the information steps still run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR paru (8),
.BR yay (8),
.BR pikaur (1),
.BR pacman (8),
.BR checkupdates (8),
.BR paccache (8),
.BR pacdiff (8),
.BR checkservices (8)
